      "question": {
        ":question/id": "de0c3d2b-8653-51d7-8456-cc8ad798ebf2",
        ":question/problem": "$\\int \\frac{1}{12-6x}\\,\\text{d}x=$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "First, let $u=12-6x.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-6\\,\u27f9\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{1}{6}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe can now rewrite the integral in terms of $u$, solve it, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{12-6x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{6}\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{6}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061|u|+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061|12-6x|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
